Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
ever since star wars the force awakens  we've seen a major uptick in fan service  that heavily utilizes nostalgia like  bringing back old characters famous  props memorable set pieces and  references and callbacks to the past the  general response to fanservice is  extremely divisive because in some cases  it can come across as exploitative and  other times it can be well integrated  ever since spider-man no way home came  out i feel like it's been a good example  as to how to do fan service correctly  although it's still not perfect so i  want to compare and contrast the good  and bad examples while going over the  three main pillars that make up great  nostalgia based fan service  the first one is that it needs to have a  purpose within the story if it's just  there for the sake of nostalgia then it  can immediately come across as  disingenuous  this is where spider-man no way home  really shines because the old villains  from the prior spider-man movies  actually have character growth obviously  not all of them have a lot of character  growth because there are 5 of them but  the best example in the group is dr  octavius we see him go from being  essentially in his villain prime of  hating spider-man to peter parker fixing  the hardware that's driving his mind  insane and him going back to being a  good person the writers don't use dr  octavius as a one-trick means to garner  nostalgia from fans these villains are a  large part of the focus and by  continuing their story in a satisfactory  way these old characters blend into the  new story to find meaning and we don't  just have character growth for dr  octavius his conflict heavily influences  peter's character arc in the movie he  learns that he'll be partially  responsible for the death of these  villains by sending them back to their  timelines and it's possible to find  another way to save their lives by  curing them which goes against the  stereotypical hero defeating the villain  stereotype so the old villains  positively impact the story because they  force peter parker to change and they  also go through new and interesting  dynamics so don't come across as a  retread like the villains interacting  amongst one another and having some cool  dialogue their interactions to seeing a  new spider-man and being in a different  universe that has new mechanics like  magic more dynamic action and having a  mostly new story also i haven't even  touched on the fact that we have  returning spider-man who get their own  redemption and closure since some of  their stories ended prematurely  comparing this to something like star  wars the force awakens the very purpose  of its own fan service is questionable  because of how much of a [ __ ] the  whole movie is  when lining up a new hope in the force  awakens the plot becomes alarmingly  identical we have one of our main  protagonists who gets ambushed by a  villain who's similar to darth vader and  before getting captured he sends away a  droid with sensitive information then  the droid happens to run into a  force-sensitive person who doesn't know  their parents and escapes off-road in  the millennium falcon they meet an old  experienced character that ushers them  into an expansive story who ends up  dying to the main villain and motivates  our heroes to take down the empire sorry  i mean the first order and destroys the  death star or starkiller base but yeah  you get the point so by crafting a story  that is almost indistinguishable to a  new hope it garners a lot of  exploitative nostalgia because they're  redoing an old story but with a new  layer of paint and all the while the  force awakens is being advertised as a  whole new story so the purpose here for  the movie seems like a cheap and  scientific way to make as much money as  possible by mining nostalgia from fans  you can kind of argue the same for  spider-man no way home but for that  movie they actually tried to do  something new meanwhile in the force  awakens a lot of the nostalgia-inducing  aspects in the movie are contrived like  the millennium falcon coincidentally  being on jakku and rey being able to  expertly fly the ship doing extremely  advanced maneuvers that not even tie  fighter pilots could keep up with and  it's also convenient that the second  they take the millennium falcon off  world they immediately bump into han  solo and chewie so they can have this  nostalgia feels good line  chewing  we're home  or even maz kanana randomly having luke  skywalker's lightsaber for some reason  and she says this where'd you get that a  good question  for another time  it's very clear that the story in this  movie comes secondary to the fan service  and since that's the case once the  nostalgia rose and the glasses effect  wears off the movie becomes much worse  and we actually see the flaws for what  they are  for the second pillar of great  fanservice it has to follow the internal  logic of the movie or in other words it  has to make sense within the established  roles of the movie's world this is where  i think spider-man know home could be  improved because the rules of the  multiverse and how dr strange's spell  works can be a bit confusing and  inconsistent like one of the main  conveniences is that the only two  spider-man and villains that show up are  people we already know as an audience in  theory there should be other renditions  of these characters looking through and  the fact that it's only characters we  are familiar with is convenient for the  story despite that being the most  practical direction to take the story  because you can't just set up a bunch of  different people we don't know so  personally this aspect does not bother  me and it's at least plausible to happen  but for other movies the writing is a  lot worse or is a straight up  contradictory for how they set up the  fan service segments the most glaring  example is ghostbusters afterlife and as  a quick warning i'll be going into some  spoilers for the movie but it's been out  for a while so if you wanted to watch it  you probably would have by now when the  main character who's aegon's daughter  finds her way into a jail cell she  decides to call the old ghostbusters  number for her one phone call which in  of itself is absurd that a child  wouldn't call their parent in this scary  situation but whatever when talking to  ray from the first movie he says this  one morning i go to work connect to one  our old cadillac is gone his new trauma  thrower collider pack all the traps 16  ounces of fuel isotope all gone he  cleaned us out but at the end of the  movie when our heroes are in need of  help peter venkman ray and winston  miraculously show up to save the day  however this moment is immediately  contradicted by what the writers told us  earlier in the movie in that phone  conversation so how did they get their  equipment back when egon supposedly  cleaned them out the writers just seemed  to hope that the audience doesn't ask  any questions and are instead blinded  and awed by the nostalgia of the  original ghostbusters being back on  screen together don't ask how these  three characters somehow got to oklahoma  within an hour's notice of the world  going to [ __ ] or let alone someone  asking these three characters for help  because that doesn't happen in the movie  they basically materialize for a  contrived entrance instead of allowing  the characters we've been following to  solve the problem themselves we're  looking at matrix resurrections which  small spoilers the writers make it to  where the machines bring back neo and  trinity from the dead because their love  for each other generates a lot of power  like what if bringing back characters is  so far-fetched that it physically  doesn't make sense within your own movie  then you need to leave them alone the  only time it's right to bring them back  is if it's purposeful and follows the  movie's internal logic otherwise it  comes across as the studio wanting to  cash in on the nostalgia for the last  pillar of great fan service you can't  rely on nostalgia-inducing references  for entertainment in small doses i think  it's fine especially if it's plausible  for the reference to be made in the  first place like when peter parker is  talking about how he'll be making cures  to save the villains and mr osmore chips  in to say this you know  i'm something of a scientist myself yes  that obviously references the meme but  it can be forgivable since the topic is  on science and he genuinely wants to  help however once you start to use  references repeatedly it drastically  lowers the new story experience because  we are constantly anchored down in the  past the worst defender of this is  ghostbusters afterlife practically a  third of the movie is our new characters  discovering old props and callbacks to  the first ghostbusters movie since our  new characters inherited egon's house  and he raided the ghostbusters and saw  all their equipment the writers send our  main characters into a scavenger hunt of  nostalgia every scene is imbued with the  essence of the leonardo dicaprio  appointing meme or the soy jack pointing  meme as he gushes about something he  just found during these scenes the plot  hits a standstill because there's no  story development in the concept of our  characters walking around and gawking at  props from the first movie and there's  an even more egregious example where  paul road is in walmart for no reason  other than to get food and he runs into  many state-puffed marshmallow men none  of the other food or products in walmart  are being affected by paranormal  activity and it's just coincidentally  the marshmallows it comes across as the  filmmakers injecting the stupid  reference for cheap nostalgia and even  worse a forced merchandising  advertisement if you thought the porgs  were bad then you'll love the mini steak  puffs marshmallows these references  should be second nature as the story  takes to priority like in spiderman no  way home the characters make references  that can easily follow the head of  casual fans while getting ready for the  third act battle toby maguire goes on  about how his back hurts and andrew  garfield offers to crack it during the  moment this comes across as brotherly  bonding between characters that  desperately need love and appreciation  in their lives to my surprise this was  actually a reference to spider-man 2  where he hurt his back and this moment  acts as a kind of easter egg so as long  as the references aren't distracting and  aren't hindering the story with a  massive nostalgia roadblock then it can  definitely work or you can go the crazy  matrix resurrections route where the  movie becomes a meta comedy about  caching on the matrix franchise where  they make tons of references the movie  weirdly becomes self-aware if you don't  break these three rules for fanservice  and it can definitely work within the  movie i'm just tired of so many  franchises falling victim to this and it  comes across as studios and writers  barely trying anymore and think that as  long as you are experiencing nostalgia  then you won't notice the movie's  glaring flaws  you

